## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 5 Critical · 0 High · 1 Medium · 0 Low** — across 8 tests

This suite has a systemic problem: **5 of 8 tests contain self-referential / tautological assertions** that can never fail regardless of whether the production code is correct. Coverage numbers from this suite are misleading.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests giving false confidence

#### 1. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77) — Always-true assertion

The input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `"HELLO".ToUpper()` is always `"HELLO"`. This test cannot fail.

```csharp
// BEFORE — tautological, input is already uppercased
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — actually tests the transformation
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

#### 2. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61) — Self-referential assertion

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` verbatim (line 10 of production code). The test asserts `user.Name == display`, which is `user.Name == user.Name`. If `GetDisplayName` were meant to format (e.g., `"Alice (30)"`), this test would still pass with the trivial implementation.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts a property equals itself through an identity method
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER — assert against an independent expected value that captures
// the intended formatting contract
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);   // or "Alice (30)" if formatting is the intent
```

---

#### 3. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42) — Self-referential assertion

`Normalize()` does `Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. Input `"alice"` is already trimmed and lowercase — the operation is identity. This test never exercises the actual transformation.

```csharp
// BEFORE — pre-normalized input, so output == input trivially
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER — test that transformation actually happens
Assert.AreEqual("alice", NameNormalizer.Normalize("  Alice  "));
Assert.AreEqual("bob",   NameNormalizer.Normalize("BOB"));
// Keep the already-normalized case as a SEPARATE, supplementary test:
Assert.AreEqual("alice", NameNormalizer.Normalize("alice"));
```

---

#### 4. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 63–69) — Self-referential + missing negative case

`ValidateEmail` returns the input on success. Asserting `input == result` is tautological for any valid input. Worse, the suite has **zero tests** for invalid input, so the `ArgumentException` path on line 64 of the production code is completely unverified.

```csharp
// BEFORE — only tests the identity happy path
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — verify the happy path with a literal, then add the negative case
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_ValidInput_ReturnsInput()
{
    var result = Validator.ValidateEmail("hello@world.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", result);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_MissingAtSign_ThrowsArgumentException()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
}
```

---

#### 5. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 44–53) — Tautological round-trip on lossy mapper

The mapper is **lossy by design**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email` and hardcodes `Age = 0`; `ToEntity` drops `Id` and `Email`. A round-trip assertion on a lossy transformation is fundamentally wrong — it either fails (because data is lost) or masks the data loss. This test should verify each mapping direction independently against expected literal values.

```csharp
// BEFORE — round-trip on a lossy mapper, masks data loss
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);       // 1 vs 0 — lossy!
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);  // "a@b.com" vs "" — lossy!

// AFTER — test each direction independently with expected literals
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);  // document the known hardcoded default
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);    // document known default
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // document known default
}
```

---

### 🟡 Medium

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Fix |
|---|------|-------------|-----|
| 6 | `Clone_ProducesEqualObject` (line 28) | Missing reference-inequality check | Add `Assert.AreNotSame(original, clone)` to verify `Clone()` actually creates a new object — without it, a `Clone()` that returns `this` would pass. |

